\noindent{\textbf{Abstract:}} Prospective forecasting of major infectious diseases suffers systematic skill degradation near epidemic thresholds and over multi-week extrapolation. This study asks how model-conditional mechanistic variance, real-world model error, and the resulting calibration gap are related. Methodologically, we prove a conditional squared-risk identity over the class of square-integrable measurable decision rules, establishing the sharp conditional variance floor attained by the conditional-mean forecast; we then build a scale-isolation mapping from individual negative-binomial branching to a macroscopic negative-binomial aggregate update model, derive an exact finite-step variance closed form for the fixed-aggregate-dispersion aggregate update model together with its numerically stable boundary limits, and give analytic expressions for lognormal parameter-posterior propagation and the plug-in predictor's excess risk. External validation uses three locked historical seasons of the CDC FluSight hub (v1.0.0, v1.1.0, v1.2.0; 204 version-locked files with registered SHA-256 checksums). The paper adopts a \emph{four-tier evidence architecture}, of which the real-data empirical component comprises three layers---individual transmission chains, the state-level hospitalization panel, and forecast-hub operations data---with theoretical simulation validation as the first tier. Simulation confirms the macro variance closed form on the tested parameter grid (relative deviation about $1\%$--$3\%$, maximum $7.6\%$). Individual transmission-chain data support the negative-binomial over-Poisson fit ($\Delta\text{AIC}$ 93.9--244.0), whereas a bootstrap-to-Cramér--Rao ratio of 0.98--1.06 is a \emph{model-internal consistency check} and does not constitute independent validation of the generating assumption. The state-level panel, using the macro aggregate update model's conditional-exact variance under a fixed-$k_{\text{agg}}$ plug-in estimate, yields a median macro mechanistic horizon of 2.2--7.1 weeks, with an unattributed closure residual of median share $55.0\%$, indicating that the mechanistic components cover only a secondary share of observed error. The \emph{retrospective final-vintage external audit}, stratified by post-hoc epidemic phase, shows that the mechanistic benchmark badly undercovers as a forecaster (nominal 95\% empirical coverage 0.405--0.554); the Hub ensemble's rising-phase coverage is 0.852, 0.589 and 0.419 across the three seasons, the official baseline 0.759, 0.539 and 0.488, and the mechanistic benchmark 0.370, 0.385 and 0.140---a substantial and season-unstable degradation of rising-phase calibration; declining-phase coverage recovers to 0.925--0.964 for the Hub ensemble, while the baseline and the mechanistic benchmark remain undercovered to differing degrees. We conclude that the mechanistic horizon is a model-conditional sharp benchmark rather than a strict upper bound on operational forecasting, and that the framework is best read as a "mechanistic benchmark--external audit--calibration gap" diagnostic tool; operational systems additionally require dynamic calibration, real-time revision modelling, and phase-specific uncertainty inflation.

